- **Shape.** On the 4 inputs every method leaves valid, the optimizer (alone or combined) has the lowest mean phi, the quantity it minimises. Winslow comes within 0.003 of it on the annulus, free disk and sphere, in 44 % of its time. By min scaled J, Laplace wins on the annulus and graded layer, the optimizer on the free disk and sphere: the scaled J reads angles only.
- **Concave outline.** The L-shape's Coons patch starts with 10 inverted quads. Laplace leaves 6, the optimizer alone 9, the combined schedule 3; Winslow leaves 1, only at the reflex corner, where two quads share a 270-degree turn.
- **Size.** No method evens element size everywhere: Laplace has the lowest spread of area (CV) on the annulus, free disk and L-shape, another method on the tangled square, sphere and graded layer.
- **Tangled squares.** Of 8 tangled squares, Laplace repaired 8, Winslow 8, the optimizer alone 0 (at the default `delta` it stops after 1 iteration), the combined schedule 8.
- **Free boundary.** The disk has `boundary="free"`: nothing snaps its boundary back. Its area changes +0.0 % under Laplace and +0.0 % under Winslow (`free_boundary="tangent"`: a boundary vertex moves only along the boundary), -15.7 % under Laplace with `free_boundary="chain"` (pulled onto the chords) and +61.7 % under the optimizer, whose scale-free metric lets the rim cells grow. Hold it with `OnCurve` or `boundary="slide"` ({doc}`constraints`).
- **Grading.** Every method thickens the graded layer: Laplace +229 %, Winslow +207 % (its guard stopped it after 3 sweeps), the optimizer +584 %; see {doc}`grading`.
- **Cost.** A Laplace pass costs 0.4 ms, a Winslow sweep 1.1 ms, an optimizer iteration 4.9 ms with its line search. Medians over the inputs; single runs on a laptop, running serial.
